# Business Services: cyber resilience, financial transmission and cross-sector exposure

**Research date:** 3 November 2026  
**Evidence cut-off:** 3 November 2026  
**Scope:** NACE divisions 62, 69, 70, 71, 73, 74, 78, 80, 81 and 82, as specified.

## Executive findings



- **Provider access can transmit an incident across clients.** Joint government guidance identifies managed service providers’ trusted connectivity and privileged access as pathways into customer networks. The Kaseya VSA incident provides a documented example of compromise through management tooling. ([nsa.gov](https://www.nsa.gov/Press-Room/News-Highlights/Article/Article/3027304/nsa-partners-issue-guidance-to-secure-managed-service-providers-their-customers/?utm_source=openai))
- **Detection is not equivalent to containment.** In Capita’s March 2023 incident, a security alert arose within ten minutes, but the affected device was not quarantined for 58 hours. The Information Commissioner’s Office (ICO) subsequently imposed combined penalties of **£14 million** in October 2025. These are findings about one organisation, not sector averages. ([ico.org.uk](https://ico.org.uk/about-the-ico/media-centre/news-and-blogs/2025/10/capita-fined-14m-for-data-breach-affecting-over-6m-people?utm_source=openai))
- **Financial effects extend beyond ransom and restoration expenditure.** Primary disclosures separately identify incident expenses, cash outflows, revenue effects and insurance arrangements at Capita, ISS, Cognizant and Sopra Steria. Their measures and observation periods differ and should not be pooled. ([capita.com](https://www.capita.com/dam/documents/investors/results-reports-presentations/2023/full-year-results-2023/Capita-plc-Annual-Report-and-Accounts-2023.pdf))
- **Recent statistics do not isolate the requested Business Services perimeter.** UK survey categories combine professional activities with scientific and technical businesses, and administrative services with real estate. Verizon separates professional services and administrative services under the North American Industry Classification System (NAICS), rather than the requested NACE divisions. ([gov.uk](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/cyber-security-breaches-survey-20252026/cyber-security-breaches-survey-20252026?utm_source=openai))





**Overall judgement:** a single Business Services risk ranking would conceal material differences. Managed service providers warrant emphasis on cross-client access; staffing on payroll, worker identity and funding continuity; legal services on confidential files and payment instructions; facilities management and security services on maintaining site-level delivery when shared information technology fails.

The evidence supports targeted control priorities and borrower-specific financial analysis. It does **not** support a defensible worldwide Business Services breach-cost average, a five-year sector cost trend, or an overall ranking against all five peer sectors.



