## A2. Prioritised capability table



The following is an **analytical prioritisation and proposed evidence-of-control benchmark**, not a measured industry percentile. “Mandatory where scoped” refers to the applicability table above; it does not mean that every implementation detail is prescribed for every provider.

| Capability | Priority subsectors | Process protected | Pathways mitigated and documented case basis | Consequence of failure | Status | Proposed benchmark |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Privileged access and client-tenant segregation | MSP/MSSP first; BPO and technical services with customer access | Remote administration and customer delivery | Management-tool compromise and lateral movement; Kaseya case | Multi-client interruption or disclosure | Mandatory where scoped under NIS2/2690 and GDPR; otherwise recommended or contractual | Separate administrative identities, bounded client access, recorded privileged sessions and demonstrated isolation |
| Multifactor authentication (MFA) and help-desk identity proofing | MSP, BPO, staffing; all privileged users | Authentication and credential resets | Stolen credentials and fraudulent resets; no provider-specific reset case established here | Account takeover, data access and payment diversion | Risk-based legal requirements where applicable; reset design recommended | Phishing-resistant authentication for privileged access; reset exercises that cannot be satisfied through caller knowledge alone |
| Alert triage and containment authority | MSP, BPO, legal; shared platforms across all subsectors | Detection-to-action workflow | Capita’s documented gap between alert and quarantine | Longer attacker access and wider impact | Incident handling mandatory where scoped; implementation risk-based | Measure alert-to-triage and alert-to-isolation separately; test authority to disconnect systems |
| Asset visibility and vulnerability management | IT, engineering, security technology and facilities technology | Exposure discovery and remediation | Internet-facing management tooling; Kaseya case | Initial compromise or interruption | Mandatory where scoped; otherwise recommended | Inventory includes remote management, software as a service (SaaS), inherited acquisitions and unsupported systems |
| Secrets management and secure transfer | Legal/accounting, IT, consulting, BPO and R&D | Tickets, files, code and client exchanges | Persistent service-account access; DPP Law case supports account-lifecycle concerns | Client-data disclosure and privileged reuse | GDPR/NIS2 duties where applicable; detailed design recommended | No reusable secrets in ordinary tickets; controlled vault access, rotation and secure transfer |
| Monitoring with client-specific visibility | MSP/MSSP and multi-client BPO | Attribution, investigation and customer assurance | Cross-client access and shared infrastructure | Inability to identify affected clients promptly | Mandatory where scoped or contracted | Logs attributable to user, tenant and administrative action; retained independently of compromised tooling |
| Incident response and client notification | All; particularly processors and multi-client providers | Containment, evidence and communications | Capita and DPP Law | Regulatory exposure, client claims and delayed containment | Mandatory notification duties where applicable; contractual duties may be earlier | Tested controller/client contact list, legal decision tree and out-of-band communications |
| Payment approval and independent call-back | Legal/accounting, staffing, BPO and facilities procurement | Payroll, client money, invoices and supplier changes | Business email compromise and payment diversion; no sector-specific loss rate established | Immediate cash loss and reconciliation disputes | Recommended; contract and professional rules may add requirements | Separate creation and approval; confirm bank changes through a previously established channel |
| Hiring and contractor identity proofing | Staffing, IT, engineering and R&D | Recruitment, onboarding and device issuance | Fraudulent remote-worker identities documented by US authorities | Unauthorised access, data theft and sanctions exposure | Recommended; employment and sanctions duties depend on jurisdiction | Match identity, location, device destination and subcontractor; investigate inconsistencies before access |
| Payroll and invoicing continuity | Staffing first; facilities, security and BPO | Time capture, wages, billing and collections | Shared-IT outages; ISS provides a facilities-management anchor | Payroll funding pressure and delayed receipts | Continuity duties where scoped; otherwise recommended | Run a payroll and invoice cycle without the main platform, then reconcile safely |
| Disaster recovery, backup integrity and recovery testing | All, tailored to service | Restoration of delivery and data | Ransomware and infrastructure rebuilds; ISS and Sopra Steria | Extended interruption, rework and unrecoverable records | Mandatory where scoped; ISO 22301 supports management approach | Restore identity, applications and data in an isolated environment; demonstrate business acceptance |
| Exit and substitution planning | MSP, BPO, payroll and embedded professional services | Transfer to another provider or in-house operation | Supplier failure or unavailable platform | Prolonged customer dependency and contract losses | DORA contractual relevance for ICT; otherwise recommended or contractual | Usable data export, documented dependencies, alternative access and a practicable transfer exercise |



## A3. Preparedness weaknesses supported by evidence



- **Legal-services account governance:** the DPP Law penalty notice identifies failures in least privilege and administrative-account review. It also records dependence on a supplier-managed legacy account. **Contextual:** the notice is dated 14 April 2025, more than 18 months before the cut-off, but remains relevant to persistent service accounts and unsupported applications. ([ico.org.uk](https://ico.org.uk/media2/pr4bg5hq/dpp-law-ltd-monetary-penalty-notice.pdf))
- **Client-provider coordination:** in an Australian Securities and Investments Commission (ASIC) in-depth review of ten financial-sector responsible entities, five had not tested their offshore provider’s incident-response capability, seven had not integrated provider contingency plans, and five did not require documented recovery timeframes. This is a **customer-side outsourcing sample**, not a prevalence estimate for Business Services providers. ([asic.gov.au](https://www.asic.gov.au/about-asic/news-centre/articles/review-of-offshore-outsourcing-responsible-entities/))
- **Supply-chain review remains an all-sector weakness:** the UK 2025/2026 survey found that 15% of businesses reviewed immediate-supplier cybersecurity risks and 6% reviewed wider supply-chain risks. These are **all-business proxies**, not Business Services benchmarks. ([gov.uk](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/cyber-security-breaches-survey-20252026/cyber-security-breaches-survey-20252026))





**Improvement areas:** demonstrate containment rather than merely detection; test complete payroll and billing cycles rather than server availability alone; maintain security ownership over legacy supplier accounts; and exercise provider-client recovery jointly. Certificates and policy documents should support, not replace, these demonstrations.



## A4. Subsector priorities



| NACE division | Leading priorities | Differentiation and evidence boundary |
|---|---|---|
| **62: IT services/MSPs** | Privileged access, client segregation, management-tool security, rapid containment and customer notification | Largest emphasis on transmission through customer access; do not equate software vendors with all MSPs |
| **69: Legal and accounting** | Confidential files, service accounts, secure transfer, payment changes and deadlines | Legal incident evidence is available; separate accounting-sector cyber-cost benchmarks were not established |
| **70: Management consulting** | Client workspaces, identity, confidential strategic data and engagement governance | Governance enforcement must be distinguished from cyber incidents |
| **71: Engineering/testing** | Project data, technical integrity, remote access and contractor requirements | Add operational-technology controls only where the provider actually connects to such systems |
| **73: Advertising/market research** | Client campaign accounts, SaaS access, research data and subcontractor access | No defensible division-specific financial benchmark established |
| **74: Other professional activities** | Data- and workflow-specific access and continuity | Too heterogeneous for an undifferentiated control or cost benchmark |
| **78: Staffing** | Payroll continuity, worker identity, bank-detail changes, timesheets and funding access | Explicitly test payment execution and recovery of billing evidence; quantified payroll-outage loss evidence remains a gap |
| **80: Security services** | Dispatch, rostering, access-control administration and separation from customer systems | Physical guarding and electronic security need different continuity tests; no primary quantified incident-cost case established |
| **81: Facilities management** | Rostering, work orders, procurement, invoicing and shared-IT recovery | ISS is a relevant individual-company case; it is not evidence that all on-site work stops during an IT outage |
| **82: Office administration/BPO/contact centres** | Client-data segregation, agent identity, secure transfer, notification and alternative delivery | Add PCI DSS only to relevant card-handling scope |

**Captive shared services/global business services:** analyse internal platform concentration, intercompany responsibilities and group liquidity support rather than assuming an external customer contract.

**R&D centres:** distinguish intellectual-property integrity and access to source code or designs from ordinary office administration. No separate defensible R&D-centre loss distribution was established.



