## B4. Credit transmission by subsector



The following are **analytical transmission pathways**, not observed sector coefficients.

| Subsector | Initial operating effect | Potential financial transmission | Borrower evidence needed |
|---|---|---|---|
| IT/MSP | Management tooling unavailable; customers suspend access | Unbillable delivery, service credits, client claims, remediation expenditure and retention pressure | Revenue by affected platform/client; liability terms; subcontractor recovery; customer-access restart approvals |
| BPO/contact centres | Client data compromised or workflow suspended | Lost transaction volumes, overtime, alternative-site costs, notification and claims | Revenue model; minimum-volume commitments; delivery-centre portability; data-access restrictions |
| Staffing | Timesheets/payroll or payment interfaces unavailable | Wages fall due before collections; billing evidence is delayed; funding availability may tighten | Payroll calendar; cash balances; receivables-finance eligibility; manual payroll controls; timesheet recovery |
| Facilities management | Shared work-order, rostering or finance systems unavailable | Overtime, missed service evidence, invoice disputes and slower cash conversion | Ability to continue on-site work; contract penalties; mobile/offline processes; client acceptance of service evidence |
| Security services | Dispatch, rostering or access administration disrupted | Replacement labour, service failure claims and customer-site restrictions | Contract type; dispatch redundancy; site procedures; separation of physical operations from corporate IT |
| Legal/accounting/consulting | Files or communications unavailable or disclosed | Lost billable time, rework, deadline exposure, client claims and retention risk | Work-in-progress, client-money arrangements, engagement terms, insurance exclusions and partner funding |
| Engineering/testing | Project systems or technical records unavailable | Project delays, re-performance, milestone-billing delays and contractual disputes | Project dependencies; testing-data integrity; delivery milestones; access to customer systems |

**Cash modelling:** separate lost contribution from deferred revenue, incremental cash expenditure, delayed collections, claim payments and insurance receipts. Avoid counting both a delayed invoice and its eventual receipt as a permanent revenue loss.

**Covenants:** test the actual contractual earnings definition. Exclusion of an incident charge from management’s adjusted earnings does not establish an equivalent covenant add-back.



## B5. Illustrative disruption scenarios



**Assumptions:** these are stress-test bands, not forecasts or sector recovery benchmarks. One, four and eight weeks describe the assumed disruption of the specified process, not necessarily complete shutdown of the company. No exact-duration case was established for most combinations.

| Band | Subsector and trigger | Anchoring case and duration boundary | Assumed operational effect and transmission | Potential offsets | Borrower data and signposts |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **1 week** | MSP tooling compromise propagating to clients | Kaseya supports the pathway, not this duration | Tool isolation; customer reconnection delayed; service credits and response costs | Separate management plane; customer-side access; clean tooling | Tenants affected; daily delivery backlog; clean-access approvals |
| **4 weeks** | Same MSP trigger | Sopra Steria supports multi-week restoration, not identical propagation or an exact four-week outage | Rebuild and phased reconnection; unbillable work and collections delay | Alternate teams; staged client recovery; contract caps where enforceable | Revenue by platform; restart milestones; customer claims |
| **8 weeks** | Same MSP trigger | No matched eight-week provider case established | Prolonged isolation; client substitution and remediation overlap | Data portability; substitute tooling; insurer-approved specialists | Renewal exposure; liquidity runway; customer exit notices |
| **1 week** | BPO data extortion | Capita supports data-loss and response consequences, not this duration | Suspend sensitive workflows; investigate client scope | Unaffected delivery centres; bounded data access | Clients/data sets affected; transaction volumes; notification decisions |
| **4 weeks** | Same BPO trigger | No matched four-week case established | Client approvals constrain restart; notification and rework costs | Alternative centre where independently operable | Backlog age; security acceptance; service-credit accruals |
| **8 weeks** | Same BPO trigger | No matched eight-week case established | Contract retention and claims become additional stress channels | Replacement workflows; negotiated transition | Termination rights; concentration; claims; cash burn |
| **1 week** | Staffing payroll-system outage | No matched primary loss case established | Manual wage calculation; delayed timesheets and invoicing | Offline roster/time records; controlled emergency payroll | Next payroll date; cash requirement; bank-file readiness |
| **4 weeks** | Same staffing trigger | No duration-matched anchor | Repeated payroll cycles precede receipts; reconciliation backlog | Client advances where agreed; available facilities | Receivables eligibility; payroll borrowing; disputed invoices |
